## Chapter 1.6: Bootstrap

“Please write your details here, and your signature here, …_Citizen_ Huo,” Nadya, who was sitting on the other side of the partition of her desk, found it hard to address him with such alienating prefix. 

With Tarasov observing from behind her, she had to remain professional. Right now, she had to be the prim and proper Militsiya on duty. Whatever bond she had with Luka this morning had to be tossed aside for impartiality.

“Citizen Huo, if the autopsy from Vladivostok arrived and it reported the same name you just told us, then I have to formally issue an arrest warrant. Do you understand and willing to comply?” Tarasov asked a question that you only have one answer.

“…Yes, Comrade Major,” Luka answered with defeat.

Luka was already putting his pen down like he put down his hope. This morning was a rollercoaster that ended up with a derailment. He was starting to regret stepping out of his dorm. The premonition was already a heavy burden, now is likely arrested and questioned for the coming week. What if something happened to Alex during then?

Nadya, on the other hand, seemed to type very slowly. It was like she was trying to buy time for something.

Then that moment arrived. An officer walked close to Tarasov to discuss the Millitsiya matter. That was when Nadya said, “Citizen Huo, please check if the information I entered is correct.”

Luka looked down to the e-paper travel ban form. Everything seemed to be correct until he noticed a new text bloomed into the paper, “Let’s talk after my shift, 1 PM, Cafe Stela.”

A hope sparked inside Luka’s heart. Nadya was still on good term with him, or at least good enough that she would want to talk. He admitted that he desperately needed someone by his side.

Luka gave Nadya an affirming nod. She quickly erased the message and then take the e-paper back and continue to copy the information down to a traditional paper per protocol like nothing conspiratorial had just happened under her superior’s watch.

After she was done, she passed the traditional copy and ballpoint pen to Luka to ink his signature. At the same time, Tarasov had just finished talking with the officer. Once he noticed that the document was completed and signed. He let out a last reminder,

“Alright, Citizen Huo, you may go back to your routine. Though, I expect your full cooperation if we decide to escalate the matter. Understood?”

“Yes, Comrade Major.” Luka replied but this time it wasn’t so defeating. 
 
Tarasov nodded before turning around and return to his investigative duty.

Nadya winked at him and he return the nod before leaving the big storm door of the precinct.

It was still 3 hours before the meetup with Nadya. He could return to his dorm and rest, but he couldn’t because…

“Little Huo!” Lin ran out of a library across the street, shouting and waving to get his attention.

She followed him here, as expected of a journalist. Normally, he would be slightly annoyed by her presence, but at this point of vulnerability, he would hug anyone who say a single Chinese word to him.

He crossed the street to meet her and was brilliantly surprised when she offered a hug first. “_Was it written on my face?_” He thought.

“Now, now, before you say anything, let’s get inside, I had enough of this cold this morning,” Lin invited.

Luka and Lin then found a place somewhere to sit and talk about what had happened starting with his vision about Alex. Lin was totally in awe the whole time while noting everything down in her laptop.

“…And that’s about it. You’re the second person I share this with,” Luka concluded his story.

“It still quite hard to believe… but you’re not the type to joke around and get yourself into trouble,” she looked at him with sincerity, “If you need my help, you at least have me, alright?”

“Thank you, Sis…” He truly valued her care.

“Compatriot need to help each other, especially in a foreign land like this. If they abuse their power here…” she smirked, “They will know what a PR disaster look like!”

She gestured, parting her hands, “Headline: Chinese Researcher Arrested in Pogranichny Based on Unverifiable ‘_Visions_.’ See how their logic can be spun against them?”

Luka let out a laugh, casually joking back, “Ain’t you supposed to be impartial?”

“Heh, who told you? Being partially impartial is the essence of journalism!”

They shared a lighthearted giggle. Luka knew that this didn’t materially helped him one bit but at least now, he didn’t feel alone anymore. 

“Well, perhaps I shouldn’t keep you here. You better go rest. Today was tough,” she advised.

“I’m afraid I can’t, Sis, I still have a meetup with this officer I told you…” He realized a bit too late to stop. He didn’t know if Nadya wanted to keep the meeting private but he had already spilled the beans to one of the worst people he could have told.

“Ooh, a meetup or a date?” She teased smiling from cheek to cheek.

“Just a meetup. We only know each other for a day. Besides, I don’t even know what’s she going to talk about,” since the cat is already out of the box, he decided to just share it all.

“Hm hm, well, since no one is going back home today…” it was written on her face, “Do you mind me joining?”

“If I say no, you would be creeping around inside or outside the cafe anyway. So, yeah, you can join,” he allowed from the lack of choices but keep something very clear, “but if she decided that it’s private then you have to promise me that you will leave us alone, okay, Sis?”

“Of course, you won’t see me for the rest of the day, I promise.”

Luka nodded then both of them get up ready to leave and travel to the cafe.

Cafe Stela is just about 30 minutes away through scheduled bus. The reason it was called like that is because it was situated close to the a polished steel monument next to the highway through Pogranichny. Stela means monument in Russian. It could get any more obvious than that. Though, the monument looked so similar to the lollipop candy, “Chupa Chups,” so some people also referred to the monument and the cafe as Chupa Chups as well.

Once they arrived, the cafe seemed to be packed with people already. After all, this was not some eclectic cafe sitting in an alley somewhere. This place attracted locals and passerby like files with its sleek architecture and decorations. They also served everything: coffee, tea, pastries, brunch, and even fine dinning on the second floor. At this point, cafe was an understatement.

“I wonder if she had made the reservation…” Seeing all the people, Luka started to feel concern about the availability.

Both of them stepped inside the cafe and were greeted by the host, “Welcome to Cafe Stela, dear guests, do you have a reservation?”

“Is there a table under the name Nadezhda Morozova?” Luka asked.

“Let me check…” The host checked her terminal, “…No, I don’t think we have a reservation under that name, I’m afraid.”

Luka and Lin looked at each other with a knowing smile, suspected as much.

“In that case, let us make a new reservation under that name.” 

“Alright. Reservation is made. Although, it would take around an hour for a spot to open up. You can wait in the snack bar in the meantime,” she gestured behind them.

“Thank you, miss,” Luka said before finding an empty spot to wait.

Good thing about an all-rounder restaurant like this was that they have an endless supply of free snack and salad for you to eat for free. Before the Time Ripple you could technically just walk in here, fill yourself up and go home without even having to order anything but most people have enough decency to not trouble the staff more than they should. Since the the Time Ripple disrupted the supply chain, the snack bar was by-order only and it deducted from your weekly food credit, though a very small amount.

After a few snacks and casual chat later, a familiar face stepped through the door. Nadya rushing-ly looked around the cafe. Luka noticed her first so he raise his hand and wave at her. Once she saw Luka she quickly rushed to the table.

“Sorry, I’m late! I even forgot to make a reservation!” She profusely apologized to him.

“Don’t worry, it’s okay, I’ve already book it for you,” he checked the time on his phone, “it should be ready soon, I hope,” it had been 45 minutes now.

Nadya unbuttoned her navy wool peacoat and place it over the empty seat. She went back home to change before coming here. Under the coat, she wore a black turtle neck and a dark blue jeans. She also let her long hair flowed naturally.

Once she sat down, she immediately asked, “May I know who is this lady?”

Luka looked at Lin, it seemed that she wanted to introduce herself, so he let her take the wheel,

“Hello! I’m Lin Chen, I’m a journalist from Heilongjiang People’s Daily and a good friend of Luka,” she extended her hand, “glad to make your acquaintance, Comrade Morozova.”

Nadya reached for a handshake, “So am I, but please call me Nadya. How about you?”

“Lin is fine, thank you,” she smiled professionally then let go of the hand.

Without knowing how much he Luka told Lin, Nadya asked vaguely, “So, um… Have you guys talked…?”

“Oh, don’t worry, I already told her pretty much everything, that’s why she’s here,” Luka affirmed and Lin nodded.

“Oh! Alright! In that case, let me get straight to the point then…” Nadya closed her eyes and took a deep breath.

After a long exhale she looked at Luka with a guilty eyes as she was saying, “First of all, I’m truly sorry, Luka. I didn’t mean to get you into trouble like that…” she completely took the blame for Luka’s impending arrest.

“Please, don’t blame yourself. None of us saw it coming,” Luka didn’t hold grudges against her at all.

“I know how strict Major Tarasov is, but I didn’t think that he would escalate that fast,”

“To be honest, I don’t blame him either. He had the right to be suspicious.” 

Nayda seemed to disagree, “Maybe. On the surface it made complete sense but on the way back to the station, I thought about our interactions so far and it stopped making sense. After all, I was the one who brought up Igor and dragged you to the crime scene, and you have no way to predict that I would do so. Then even after getting close to the crime scene, you didn’t cross the cordon or tamper with anything. Then you revealed the critical information that obviously discriminated yourself,” Nadya explained.

“If you really were involved in this then it would be the stupidest plan ever. I doubt someone who started a fire to make a body decoy to fool the Militsiya would do something like this,” Nadya concluded her rationale.

Luka nodded along. It was nice to know that despite everything, his interaction with her ended up being the source of her trust in him.
 
Their conversation interrupted by the host calling out their queue number. So they got up and relocated to the guest table.

After ordering some drinks and pastries, Lin started,

“Despite our trust in Luka, the clock had already been ticking. Do you know how long until the autopsy arrived?” 

Nadya thoughts briefly before answered, “It’s hard too tell, but it shouldn’t be more than two days…”

Two days were not at all a long time. Their trust was nothing against an empirical evidence. The realization brought down the mood once again.

“Perhaps we can find something that would prove Luka’s innocence?” Lin suggested.

“Well, I do believe that they won’t find anything connecting Luka to the crime but we should try to prevent things from escalating.” 

“Yeah, I think so too,” Lin turned to Luka and asked, “Do you have alibi? Do you have anyone or anything that confirms your whereabouts last night?” 

“Nothing, I’m afraid. The CCTV around my dorm are still down and I was sleeping, alone, per usual.” he made a self-deprecating jokes, trying to ease the tension but didn’t land well.

The table went into an awkward dead air for a moment before Nadya spoke up,

“Hey! I have an idea. Let me…” she searched her leather cross-body bag and picked up a roll of e-paper. She unrolled it to check to check its content before saying,

“Alright, Luka. I don’t want to abuse your power but let’s say that I swear on my mother’s life that I will write the future case summary onto this e-paper,” she confidently voiced out her ultimate plan, “If you see anything useful on the paper then it might give us some advantages.”

If what she proposed was possible then Luka might be the most influential person on earth. Though, seeing into the future with absolute certainty is scary, at least for him. Being a theorist is about proving and developing hypothesis via countless trials and errors. Knowing a definite truth right away defeated the purpose entirely. What ironic was that by actively developing the understanding for his vision, Luka got closer and closer to that reality. 

“_We never know if we never try…_” Luka thought as he focused and stared blankly into the e-paper, letting his vision melted away…






